---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import PlantDivSim from '../../simulators/plantdiv/PlantDivSim.astro';
import { plantDiversityQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/plant-diversity';

const toc = [
  { id: 'land', label: 'Plants move onto land' },
  { id: 'groups', label: 'The four main groups' },
  { id: 'bryophytes', label: 'Bryophytes' },
  { id: 'ferns', label: 'Ferns and their allies' },
  { id: 'gymnosperms', label: 'Gymnosperms' },
  { id: 'angiosperms', label: 'Angiosperms' },
  { id: 'alternation', label: 'Alternation of generations' },
  { id: 'trends', label: 'Trends in plant evolution' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the simulator simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapters 25–26: Seedless Plants; Seed Plants. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/25-introduction' },
  { text: 'Raven, P. H., Evert, R. F., & Eichhorn, S. E. (2013). Biology of Plants (8th ed.). W. H. Freeman.' },
  { text: 'Kenrick, P., & Crane, P. R. (1997). The origin and early evolution of plants on land. Nature, 389, 33–39.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1038/37918' },
  { text: 'Christenhusz, M. J. M., & Byng, J. W. (2016). The number of known plant species in the world and its annual increase. Phytotaxa, 261(3), 201–217.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.11646/phytotaxa.261.3.1' },
  { text: 'Morris, J. L., et al. (2018). The timescale of early land plant evolution. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 115(10), E2274–E2283.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1719588115' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="botany"
  topic="plant-diversity"
  description="Interactive plant diversity simulator: sort mosses, ferns, conifers and flowering plants on an evolutionary tree, step through alternation of generations in each group, and test why mosses and ferns need water to reproduce."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={plantDiversityQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A carpet of moss, a giant tree fern, a pine forest and a field of flowers look very different, but they share one long
      family history. Over almost half a billion years, plants invented new ways to carry water, protect their young and
      reproduce without getting wet. Sort plants into their groups, step through their surprising double life cycles, and see
      why mosses and ferns still need water to reproduce.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <PlantDivSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>In <strong>Plant groups</strong>, drag each of the 12 specimens onto its group on the tree. Tap a group or a yellow innovation for an explanation. Wrong guesses give a clue.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Life cycles</strong>, choose a moss, fern, pine or flowering plant and step through its stages with <strong>Next stage</strong>, or press <strong>Start</strong>. Blue stages are haploid (n) and orange stages diploid (2n).</li>
      <li>Compare which generation is dominant in each plant, and where meiosis and fertilisation happen.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Water</strong>, lower the water on the ground, change the wind, remove the insects, or let the sun dry the ground, and see which plants can still be fertilised.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset</strong> shuffles the specimens and restores every setting.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="land">Plants move onto land</h2>
  <p>
    Land plants evolved from green algae and first appear in the fossil record about 470 million years ago. Life on land
    offered bright light and plenty of carbon dioxide, but brought new problems: drying out, holding the body up without
    the support of water, and getting sperm to eggs. Early land plants had a waxy <strong>cuticle</strong>, spores with
    tough walls, and protected their embryos inside the parent plant. That last feature is why land plants are also called
    embryophytes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="groups">The four main groups</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Key features</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Bryophytes</strong></td><td>No vascular tissue; spores; gametophyte dominant</td><td>Mosses, liverworts, hornworts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ferns and allies</strong></td><td>Vascular tissue; spores; sporophyte dominant</td><td>Ferns, horsetails, clubmosses</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gymnosperms</strong></td><td>Seeds on cone scales; pollen</td><td>Pines, spruces, cycads, ginkgo</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Angiosperms</strong></td><td>Flowers; seeds inside fruits</td><td>Grasses, oaks, roses, orchids</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bryophytes">Bryophytes</h2>
  <p>
    Mosses, liverworts and hornworts (about 20,000 species) have no lignified <strong>vascular tissue</strong>. Water moves
    from cell to cell or over the surface, so they stay small, usually only a few centimetres tall. Root-like
    <strong>rhizoids</strong> anchor them but are not true roots. The green plant is the haploid
    <strong>gametophyte</strong>. Its sperm swim through rain or dew to the eggs, and the resulting sporophyte, a stalk
    and capsule, grows on top of the gametophyte and depends on it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ferns">Ferns and their allies</h2>
  <p>
    About 430 million years ago, plants evolved <strong>vascular tissue</strong>: xylem with walls strengthened by
    <strong>lignin</strong>, and phloem. Plants could now carry water from true roots to leaves held high in the air, and
    the first forests grew. Ferns, horsetails and clubmosses (about 13,000 species) still reproduce with
    <strong>spores</strong>. The fern plant is the diploid sporophyte; brown <strong>sori</strong> under the fronds release
    spores, which grow into a tiny, independent gametophyte, the <strong>prothallus</strong>. Its sperm, too, must swim.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gymnosperms">Gymnosperms</h2>
  <p>
    Seed plants appeared about 360 million years ago. Their male gametophytes are packed into <strong>pollen</strong>
    grains carried by the wind, so fertilisation no longer needs a film of water. A <strong>seed</strong> contains an
    embryo, a food store and a protective coat. In gymnosperms (“naked seeds”; about 1,100 species, including pines,
    spruces, cycads and ginkgo), the seeds sit on the surface of cone scales rather than inside a fruit. Conifers dominate
    cold northern forests and include the tallest and oldest living trees.
  </p>

  <h2 id="angiosperms">Angiosperms</h2>
  <p>
    Flowering plants appeared about 140 million years ago and now make up roughly 90% of land plant species, around
    300,000 in all. Their <strong>ovules are enclosed in an ovary</strong>, which develops into a <strong>fruit</strong>
    that protects and spreads the seeds. <strong>Flowers</strong> attract insects, birds and bats that carry pollen
    accurately from flower to flower. Grasses and most broad-leaved trees, such as oaks, are flowering plants with small,
    wind-pollinated flowers. How pollination and double fertilisation work is explored in the Plant Reproduction topic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alternation">Alternation of generations</h2>
  <p>
    Every land plant’s life cycle alternates between two multicellular generations:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>gametophyte</strong> is haploid (n). It makes gametes (sperm and eggs) by <strong>mitosis</strong>.</li>
    <li>Two gametes fuse at <strong>fertilisation</strong> to make a diploid (2n) zygote, which grows into the <strong>sporophyte</strong>.</li>
    <li>The sporophyte makes haploid <strong>spores</strong> by <strong>meiosis</strong>, and each spore grows into a new gametophyte.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The balance between the generations shifted during evolution. In mosses the gametophyte is the main plant. In ferns
    the sporophyte is the main plant but the gametophyte still lives independently. In seed plants the gametophytes are
    reduced to a few cells hidden inside pollen grains and ovules, protected by the sporophyte.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trends">Trends in plant evolution</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>From small plants without vascular tissue to tall plants with lignified xylem.</li>
    <li>From a dominant gametophyte to a dominant sporophyte.</li>
    <li>From swimming sperm to pollen, freeing reproduction from water.</li>
    <li>From single-celled spores to seeds, and then to seeds protected inside fruits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="applications">Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Food</strong>: almost all crops, including wheat, rice and maize (all grasses), are flowering plants.</li>
    <li><strong>Timber and paper</strong>: much of it comes from conifers.</li>
    <li><strong>Coal</strong>: formed from the remains of giant clubmoss and horsetail forests of the Carboniferous period, around 300 million years ago.</li>
    <li><strong>Peat bogs</strong>: built by sphagnum moss, they store a huge amount of carbon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Clubmosses, Spanish moss and reindeer moss are mosses.”</strong> They are a vascular plant, a flowering plant and a lichen.</li>
    <li><strong>“Trees are not flowering plants.”</strong> Oaks, maples and most broad-leaved trees flower; conifers do not.</li>
    <li><strong>“Spores and seeds are the same.”</strong> A spore is a single cell; a seed contains a multicellular embryo with food and a coat.</li>
    <li><strong>“Pollen is sperm.”</strong> A pollen grain is a tiny male gametophyte that produces sperm cells.</li>
    <li><strong>“Ferns have seeds on their leaves.”</strong> The brown dots are sori, which release spores.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the simulator simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The tree shows only four broad groups and a few key innovations; real plant relationships are more detailed, and
    flowering plants and gymnosperms evolved from extinct seed-plant ancestors not shown here. Dates are approximate first
    appearances in the fossil record and species counts are rounded estimates. The life cycles are summarised in five or
    six stages, and drawings are not to scale. In the water experiment the threshold for a “continuous water film” is
    arbitrary, the flower is assumed to be insect-pollinated, and the pine is pollinated only by wind. Some gymnosperms
    (cycads and ginkgo) actually still have swimming sperm, released inside the ovule.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Land plants evolved from algae about 470 million years ago and split into four main groups.</li>
    <li>Vascular tissue let plants grow tall; seeds and pollen freed them from water; flowers and fruits brought animal partners.</li>
    <li>All land plants alternate between a haploid gametophyte and a diploid sporophyte.</li>
    <li>The gametophyte is dominant in mosses; the sporophyte is dominant in ferns and seed plants.</li>
    <li>Mosses and ferns need water for their swimming sperm; seed plants use pollen.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
